Let $M^n$ be a smooth, closed, connected manifold of dimension $n \geq 2$. A diffeomorphism $f: M 	o M$ is called partially hyperbolic if there exists a $Df$-invariant continuous splitting of the tangent bundle
$$TM = E^s \oplus E^c \oplus E^u,$$
with all three subbundles of positive dimension, together with a Riemannian metric on $M$ and continuous positive functions $
u, \hat{
u}, \gamma, \hat{\gamma}$ with $
u, \hat{
u} < 1$ and $
u < \gamma \leq \hat{\gamma}^{-1} < \hat{
u}^{-1}$, such that for every $x \in M$ and unit vectors $v^s \in E^s_x$, $v^c \in E^c_x$, $v^u \in E^u_x$ one has
$$\|Df(x)v^s\| \leq 
u(x), \quad \gamma(x) \leq \|Df(x)v^c\| \leq \hat{\gamma}(x)^{-1}, \quad \hat{
u}(x)^{-1} \leq \|Df(x)v^u\|.$$

Question: Does there exist a closed, connected, simply connected manifold $M^n$ (in some dimension $n$) that admits a partially hyperbolic diffeomorphism?
